{"key":"R0292","title":"Continuous Marine Monitoring via Autonomous UAV Handoff","printed_title":"Continuous Marine Tracking via Autonomous UAV Handoff","authors":"Heegyeong Kim, Alice James, Avishkar Seth, Endrowednes Kuantama, Jane Williamson, Yimeng Feng, Richard Han","year":2025,"venue":"Proceedings of the 23rd Annual International Conference on Mobile Systems, Applications and Services","doi":"10.1145/3711875.3737661","version":"PUBLISHER_VERSION (MobiSys '25 ACM proceedings PDF; PDF p. 1 prints 'Continuous Marine Tracking via Autonomous UAV Handoff' with DOI 10.1145/3711875.3737661, whereas the publisher deposit at https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1145/3711875.3737661 records 'Continuous Marine Monitoring via Autonomous UAV Handoff', published-online 2025-09-25)","support":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","page":1,"section":"Title, Byline, and ACM Footer","quote":"Continuous Marine Tracking via Autonomous UAV Handoff ... MobiSys '25, June 23-27, 2025, Anaheim, CA, USA © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s). ACM ISBN 979-8-4007-1453-5/2025/06. https://doi.org/10.1145/3711875.3737661"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/supplemental_screening/batch_07_inputs.json","page":0,"section":"R0292 (https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1145/3711875.3737661)","quote":"title=Continuous Marine Monitoring via Autonomous UAV Handoff; year=2025; venue=Proceedings of the 23rd Annual International Conference on Mobile Systems, Applications and Services; date.type=published-online; date.value=2025-09-25"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/supplemental_screening/batch_07_review.md","page":0,"section":"R0292 adjudication note","quote":"The publisher-deposited exact DOI, author list and proceedings span match Macquarie's record for the final ACM PDF, which explains the different printed title and labels this record peer-reviewed."}]}
{"key":"R0036/R0048","decision":"DISTINCT","reason":"R0036 and R0048 implement different system architectures, algorithms, and evaluation designs. R0036 studies UAV-to-ground-edge-server video configuration control (SAC^2), combining a neural scene-adaptive accuracy profiler (target size and optical-flow velocity) with greedy k-swap submodular optimization for UAV selection, frame rate, and resolution to maximize effective surveillance coverage, evaluated on the ETH pedestrian detection dataset (1,804 frames, 10-second chunks, 900 grids) using YOLOv5 at the edge server against FC, CBA, AU, RU, and OPT baselines. R0048 studies inter-UAV collaborative video analytics (UCAA) with classifier-level DAG partitioning using centralized GA+DP+convex optimization (JDTSO) and distributed MAPPO+DAG partitioning (MAPDP), evaluated in a 500m x 500m PyTorch/OpenCV simulation of 5 to 200 heterogeneous UAVs modeled on Raspberry Pi 3 B+ with Intel Movidius NCS1/NCS2 running YOLOv3-tiny classifiers against C-PPO, C-DQN, MADDPG, Random, and IND-UAV on latency, energy, and throughput.","support":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0036.pdf","page":10,"section":"VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION","quote":"We use the widely-adopted "ETH" pedestrian detection dataset [40] for video analytics, which contains 1,804 images in three video clips captured from a stereo rig mounted on car"},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0048.pdf","page":9,"section":"VII-A. Methodology (2) Simulation Setup","quote":"We use two types of UAVs, each equipped with a Raspberry Pi 3 B+ and an Intel Movidius Neural Computing Stick (NCS)."}]}
{"key":"R0145/R0223","decision":"DISTINCT","reason":"R0145 and R0223 differ in core planning mechanisms, model architectures, and evaluation setups. R0145 fine-tunes local quantized small LLMs (LLaMA-3.2-3B) and cloud large LLMs (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B) via LoRA/Unsloth on 20,000 multi-step deep thinking samples (MSDTMD-SG) with scene memory replay to output direct discrete waypoint decisions from symbolic local grids, evaluated on a 20m x 20m discrete grid rescue scenario with 10 UAVs and 8% obstacles against DQN-based DRL-USPP and ACO-USPP. R0223 combines a server-side Doubao LLM (UTTUT loop and P^3 factor graph) for natural-language task-graph decomposition, on-drone Grounding DINO RGB-D target perception, and an improved CTDE parameter-sharing PPO path planner, evaluated across MATLAB 3D obstacle-avoidance runs (3 and 6 UAVs, 6 and 9 obstacles vs. PPO, DDPG, SAC) and Microsoft AirSim scenarios (Obstacle Course, Wind Farm, Residential Courtyard) comparing Centralization, Parallelism, Distribution, and Master coordination frameworks.","support":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":14,"section":"6.1 Experiment settings","quote":"The UAV swarm consists of 10 UAVs, and their initial positions are randomly initialized and follow a discrete uniform distribution. We set 8% of the grids as obstacles and place the rescue point (destination) at the top-right corner of the scenario."},{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","page":4,"section":"4 Experience","quote":"The simulation environment was based on Microsoft AirSim, and three representative scenarios were selected: obstacle course [19.], wind farm [17.], and residential courtyard [17.] as shown in Figure 4."}]}
